course: dictionary
lesson: 7343
title: Plasticity: shaped, or changing
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Plasticity is a noun with two meanings. The first is about shape: clay can be squashed and moulded into a bowl, and it keeps that new shape. The second is about living things: the ability to change and adapt.
explain: In science, plasticity is a property of clay, putty and some metals. In lessons about the brain, brain plasticity means the brain can change. It makes new connections when we practise, which is why practice helps. «Brains change as you learn, which is called plasticity, especially fast in childhood.»
explain: Use the context to choose the meaning. The sentence is about learning and brains, so plasticity must mean the brain's ability to change, not the shape of clay. Test it by putting your meaning in the sentence: "Brains can change as you learn" still makes sense, so you have it.
fact: Plasticity means being able to be shaped easily and keep the new shape, like clay.
fact: In science, plasticity means the ability of the brain or a living thing to change and adapt.
fact: Brain plasticity means the brain makes new connections when we practise, which is why practice helps.
fact: «Brains change as you learn, which is called plasticity, especially fast in childhood.»
fact: «London taxi drivers who learned all the streets were found to have a bigger memory area.» This is an example of brain plasticity.
exercise: «Brains change as you learn, which is called plasticity.» What does plasticity mean here? | Very old and fixed for ever | *Being able to change and adapt, as the brain does when learning | Being made of plastic, like a bottle | Very loud
exercise: Clay can be squashed, moulded and keep its new shape. Which word from this lesson describes this? | *Plasticity | Gravity | Curt | Luminous
exercise: Why does practice help the brain? | Because the brain only works in old age | *Because of brain plasticity: the brain makes new connections when we practise | Because practice makes the brain stop forming connections | Because the brain stops changing once we are nine
test: Which meaning of plasticity matches clay being moulded in a pottery lesson? | *Able to be moulded easily and keep the new shape | Giving out light, or glowing | Very old | Hung down in sadness
test: «Brain plasticity means the brain makes new connections when we practise.» Why is practice useful? | Because practice stops the brain from learning | Because practice keeps the brain exactly the same | Because the brain only works in old age | *Because the brain can change and form new connections
exam: Which clue tells you that plasticity means the brain's ability to change? | The word is printed in bold | It has nine letters | The sentence is about cooking dinner | *The sentence is about learning and brains
